# sha256. A full sha256 of a ~240MB cmd binary cost ~1.2-1.5s CPU and this
# check runs multiple times per invocation (cache-hit decision + pre-exec
# revalidation) — several seconds of CPU spent re-hashing bytes that did not
# change. The Go build id is a content-derived fingerprint the toolchain
# already embeds in a small ELF note near the head of the file (ULTRAPLAN
# invocation-ceremony fix); reading it plus the inode/size/mtime tuple is
# O(1) in the binary's size and still fails closed on truncation, corruption,
# stale artifacts or a rebuild. This is NOT the wall-clock/mtime-only
# heuristic rejected below for `cached_binary_fast_path_valid` — that ordering
# heuristic trusts "newer" timestamps, which restored caches routinely fake.
# Here mtime/inode/size are combined with a build-id equality check, i.e.
# authenticated identity, not ordering. The cache directory and every
# ancestor are already authenticated as current-user-owned and
# non-group/world-writable (see the cache_ancestor walk above), so the only
# actor able to swap this binary could equally rewrite the recorded digest;
# a full content hash buys nothing extra against that actor and only costs
# CPU against the legitimate case (unchanged binary).
